<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <!-- 🔴 EMPTY ON PURPOSE — and this is the build flag.

       A local Expo module's manifest is merged into the app's unconditionally, so anything
       declared here ships in every build. The <service> that registers
       PhiloiFocusNudgeAccessibilityService, the SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW permission, and the <queries>
       allow-list therefore live in plugins/withFocusNudgeAndroid.js instead, which app.config.ts
       only applies when FOCUS_NUDGE_ANDROID=1.

       Why it has to work that way: Play decides an app "uses the AccessibilityService API" — and
       so requires the sensitive-permission declaration and its multi-week extended review — by
       reading the MANIFEST, not the JS. A feature flag in src/constants/feature-flags.ts would
       hide the UI and change nothing about the review. The closed test (12 testers x 14 days) must
       not be gated on that review, so the closed-test build has to be able to ship with no trace of
       the service in its manifest at all.

       With the flag off, the Kotlin classes still compile into the APK. That is fine and it is the
       line worth understanding: an accessibility service class that no <service> element registers
       is unreachable code — the system can never bind it, hasAccessibility() can never find its
       component in the enabled list, and arm() therefore returns false. Nothing to declare. -->
</manifest>
